  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What do gut bacteria do?",
      "a": "They break down fibre we cannot digest, make useful substances like short-chain fatty acids and vitamin K, crowd out harmful germs, and help train the immune system.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the gut bacteria",
        "What is the gut microbiome?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How many bacteria live in your gut?",
      "a": "An estimated 38 trillion or so bacteria live in the human body, mostly in the large intestine, roughly equal to the number of human cells.",
      "asks": [
        "Are there more bacteria than cells in the body?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are short-chain fatty acids?",
      "a": "They are small molecules, such as butyrate, made when gut bacteria ferment fibre. The cells lining the large intestine use butyrate as their main energy source.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the fatty acids",
        "What is butyrate?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do gut bacteria help the immune system?",
      "a": "Living alongside friendly bacteria from early life helps the immune system learn which microbes are harmless and which to attack, and some bacteria release signals that keep immune responses balanced.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about immune training"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do beans make gas?",
      "a": "Beans contain sugars and fibre we can't digest. Gut bacteria ferment them in the large intestine and release gases such as hydrogen and carbon dioxide.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does fibre cause wind?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A 2016 estimate put the number of bacteria in an average human body at about 38 trillion, similar to the number of human cells.",
    "Butyrate, made by gut bacteria from fibre, is the main fuel of cells lining the colon."
